Finding a Common Denominator
Recall how we add fractions:
\[\frac{2}{x}+\frac{3}{x+1}
=\frac{2(x+1)+3x}{x(x+1)}
=\frac{5x+2}{x(x+1)}\]

Mass Density Example 1
Let \(\rho(x)=\sqrt{x}\) be the radial density of a disk of radius \(4\). Find the mass of this disk.

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{mass}
&=\int_0^4 2\pi x\rho(x)\,dx\\
&=\frac{128\pi}{5}
\end{aligned}
\]

Mass Density Example 2
Let \(\rho(x)=2-|x|\) the density of a thin plate as a function of \(x\). Suppost the shape is the region bounded by \(f(x)= 4-x^2\) and the \(x\)-axis. What is the mass of this plate?

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{mass}
&=\int_{-2}^2 (2-|x|) (4-x^2)\,dx\\
&=\frac{40}{3}
\end{aligned}
\]

Surface Area Example 1
Let \(f(x)=\sqrt{x}\) on \([1,4]\).
Find the surface area generated by revolving the graph around the \(x\)-axis.

\(f'(x)=\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}\)


\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Surface Area}
&=\int_1^4
2\pi\sqrt{x}
\sqrt{1+\frac{1}{4x}}\,dx\\
&=\int_1^4
2\pi\sqrt{x+\frac14}\,dx.
\end{aligned}
\]
. . . \[
\boxed{
\text{Surface Area}
=
\frac{\pi}{6}
\left(17\sqrt{17}-5\sqrt5\right)
}
\]

Surface Area Example 2
Let \(y=\sqrt[3]{3x}\), for \(0\le y\le2\), and revolve the curve around the \(y\)-axis.

Rewrite the curve as a function of \(y\): \(x=g(y)=\dfrac13y^3\).


\(g'(y)=y^2\).
Thus,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Surface Area}
&=\int_0^2
2\pi\left(\frac13y^3\right)
\sqrt{1+y^4}\,dy\\
&=\frac{2\pi}{3}
\int_0^2 y^3\sqrt{1+y^4}\,dy.
\end{aligned}
\]


\[
\boxed{
\text{Surface Area}
=
\frac{\pi}{9}
\left(17^{3/2}-1\right)
}
\]
